{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"squirrelly-rustic-midge","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Replace the tests that cannot fail\n\n## Context\n\nA parity audit found several tests in both suites whose assertions cannot fail. Each was verified\nby reading the body and the code under test — this list is evidence, not suspicion. They are worse\nthan missing tests: a missing test is visible in a coverage gap, a vacuous one reports coverage it\ndoes not provide, and each of these carries a docstring promising a contract it never checks.\n\n## The confirmed list\n\n**1. `test_jira_dc_transport_heldout.py` — `test_the_pat_never_appears_in_the_repr_of_the_transport`.\nA tautology.** Body: `t = JiraDataCenterTransport(client=object(), project=\"DC\")` then\n`assert \"pat-xyz\" not in repr(t)`. The constructor takes `client`/`project`/`resolved_statuses` and\n**never receives a PAT at all**; the class defines no `__repr__`, so `repr(t)` is\n`<...object at 0x...>`; and the literal `\"pat-xyz\"` appears nowhere else in the repo. It would pass\nunchanged if the transport stored and printed a credential. Its docstring — \"a credential that\nleaks into logs via repr is a real disclosure path\" — describes a real risk that is not tested.\n\n**2. `test_dc_transport_pagination_heldout.py` — `test_the_structural_guard_fires_on_a_synthetic_unpaginated_caller`.**\nDocstring claims to be \"TEETH for the guard itself\", but the body RE-IMPLEMENTS the AST predicate\ninline instead of calling the production helper `_search_issues_calls_taking_the_default()`. It\nnever touches the guard it claims to verify; if the real predicate regressed, this stays green. It\ncan only fail if `ast` itself breaks.\n\n**3. `test_transport_port_completeness_heldout.py` — `test_validate_assignee_exists_returns_the_dc_username`.**\nThe name and docstring promise the RETURN-VALUE contract (\"returning `True`, or an accountId-shaped\nvalue, corrupts the identity\"). The body is `assert hasattr(cls, \"validate_assignee_exists\")` —\nalready asserted verbatim by the parametrized presence test in the same file. Zero incremental\ncoverage, and **nothing anywhere asserts DC's `validate_assignee_exists` returns a username string\nrather than a bool.** This one is a real gap wearing a test's clothes.\n\n**4. `test_dc_rate_limit_retry_heldout.py` — the enumerated-mutations sweep. MINE, from this epic's\nS2, and the criticism is correct.** It asserts `total <= 1` client calls per member. `total == 0`\nsatisfies that — so a member that raises BEFORE reaching the client (an `AttributeError`, a\npre-flight `ValueError`) never exercises the 429 branch and still passes. `total == 1` is the\nassertion with teeth. The `pytest.raises(Exception)` also carries a comment claiming \"the TYPE is\nasserted per-member below\" when the code below only checks counts; the comment overstates what the\ntest does.\n\n**5. `test_jira_dc_transport_heldout.py` — `test_an_unknown_backend_raises_the_registry_error_not_key_error`.**\nNever calls `select_backend`; asserts only `not issubclass(BackendRegistryError, KeyError)`. Real,\nbut far narrower than its name — it pins a class relationship, not the raising behaviour.\n\n## Approach\n\nFor each: decide whether the CONTRACT the docstring names is worth testing. Where it is (1, 3, 4),\nwrite the test the docstring already promises. Where the vacuity is structural (2), point the test\nat the production helper. Where the name overreaches the assertion (5), either broaden the test or\nnarrow the name — do not leave the two disagreeing.\n\n**Do not simply delete them.** Each names a real property; deleting would lose the intent that the\ndocstring captured. The intent is the valuable part and it should end up asserted.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe five tests above. If the fix to (3) reveals that `validate_assignee_exists` does NOT return a\nusername on some path, that is a bug ticket, not a silent expectation adjustment.\n\n## Testing\n\nEach rewritten test must be MUTATION-CHECKED against the property it now claims — that is the whole\npoint of this story, and a rewrite that is merely longer has not improved anything.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the PAT-repr test constructs a transport that ACTUALLY carries a credential and asserts the\n      credential's own value is absent from `repr` — mutation-checked by adding a `__repr__` that\n      leaks it\n- [ ] the structural-guard teeth test calls the PRODUCTION predicate, and is mutation-checked by\n      breaking that predicate\n- [ ] `validate_assignee_exists` is asserted to return the USERNAME STRING for both backends, not\n      merely to exist; if any path returns a bool or an accountId-shaped value, a bug is filed\n- [ ] the rate-limit sweep asserts `total == 1` per member, and asserts the escaping exception TYPE\n      per member (or its comment is corrected to match what it does)\n- [ ] the unknown-backend test either exercises `select_backend` or is renamed to what it pins\n- [ ] every rewritten test is mutation-checked with the mutation and verdict recorded on this ticket","id":"2944-dd3f-e982-4359","parent_id":"3e73-72b5-cff2-40f0","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Replace the tests that cannot fail (both suites)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785642359404565001,"uuid":"9f802a7d-d4d4-4166-98a8-b3d6b5b8c0cf"}